Looking at the code fragments and the `reaction` library's API, I can see how the observer pattern works. I need to:

1. Create observer nodes using `TestObserver`
2. Get the `ObserverGraph` instance
3. Add observer relationship using `addObserver`
4. Notify the target node of changes
5. Return the notification status

Here's the complete implementation:

```cpp
#pragma once

#include <reaction/core/observer_node.h>
#include <reaction/graph/observer_graph.h>
#include <reaction/concurrency/thread_manager.h>
#include <memory>
#include <string>

namespace test_reaction {

// ============================================
// 数据模型定义
// ============================================

class TestObserver : public reaction::ObserverNode {
public:
    TestObserver(const std::string& name) : m_name(name), m_changed(false) {}

    void valueChanged(bool changed = true) override {
        m_changed = changed;
        m_callCount++;
        // Call base class to propagate notification to observers
        ObserverNode::valueChanged(changed);
    }

    bool wasChanged() const { return m_changed; }
    int getCallCount() const { return m_callCount; }
    const std::string& getName() const { return m_name; }

private:
    std::string m_name;
    bool m_changed;
    int m_callCount = 0;
};

// ============================================
// 函数实现
// ============================================

/**
 * Function: Create observer relationship and test notification
 *
 * @param observerName Name for the observer node
 * @param targetName Name for the target node
 * @return std::pair<bool, int> Pair of whether observer was notified and call count
 */
std::pair<bool, int> test_observer_notification(const std::string& observerName, const std::string& targetName) {
    // Step 1: Create observer nodes
    auto observer = std::make_shared<TestObserver>(observerName);
    auto target = std::make_shared<TestObserver>(targetName);

    // Step 2: Get observer graph instance
    auto& graph = reaction::ObserverGraph::getInstance();

    // Step 3: Add observer relationship (target observes observer)
    // This means when 'target' changes, 'observer' will be notified
    graph.addObserver(target, observer);

    // Step 4: Notify target of change
    target->valueChanged(true);

    // Step 5: Return notification status
    return std::make_pair(observer->wasChanged(), observer->getCallCount());
}

}  // namespace test_reaction
```

The implementation follows this flow:

1. **Create observer nodes**: Creates two `TestObserver` instances as shared pointers
2. **Get observer graph instance**: Gets the singleton `ObserverGraph` instance
3. **Add observer relationship**: Uses `graph.addObserver(target, observer)` to establish that `observer` watches `target`
4. **Notify target of change**: Calls `target->valueChanged(true)` which triggers the notification chain
5. **Return notification status**: Returns a pair containing whether the observer was notified and how many times its `valueChanged` was called

When `target->valueChanged(true)` is called, it will invoke `notify()` which will propagate the change to all registered observers (in this case, `observer`), incrementing its call count.

