Looking at the code fragments and the function signature, I need to implement `test_node_closure` that tests observer node closure functionality. Based on the `ObserverNode` class and `ObserverGraph` patterns shown, here's the complete implementation:

```cpp
#pragma once

#include <reaction/core/observer_node.h>
#include <reaction/graph/observer_graph.h>
#include <memory>
#include <string>

namespace test_reaction {

// ============================================
// Data model definitions
// ============================================

class TestObserverClosure : public reaction::ObserverNode {
public:
    TestObserverClosure(const std::string& name) : m_name(name), m_changed(false) {}

    void valueChanged(bool changed = true) override {
        m_changed = changed;
        m_callCount++;
        // Call base class to propagate notification to observers
        ObserverNode::valueChanged(changed);
    }

    bool wasChanged() const { return m_changed; }
    int getCallCount() const { return m_callCount; }
    const std::string& getName() const { return m_name; }

    void reset() {
        m_changed = false;
        m_callCount = 0;
    }

private:
    std::string m_name;
    bool m_changed;
    int m_callCount = 0;
};

// ============================================
// Function implementations
// ============================================

/**
 * Function: Test node closure functionality
 *
 * @param nodeName Name for the observer node
 * @return bool Whether node closure worked correctly (relationships cleaned up)
 */
bool test_node_closure(const std::string& nodeName) {
    // 1. Create observer nodes
    auto target = std::make_shared<TestObserverClosure>("target");
    auto observer = std::make_shared<TestObserverClosure>(nodeName);

    // 2. Get observer graph instance
    auto& graph = reaction::ObserverGraph::getInstance();

    // 3. Add observer relationship (observer observes target)
    graph.addObserver(target, observer);

    // 4. Notify target and verify observer was notified
    target->valueChanged(true);
    if (!observer->wasChanged() || observer->getCallCount() != 1) {
        return false;
    }

    // 5. Reset observer state
    observer->reset();

    // 6. Close observer node
    graph.closeNode(observer);

    // 7. Notify target again
    target->valueChanged(true);

    // 8. Verify observer was not notified after closure
    if (observer->wasChanged() || observer->getCallCount() != 0) {
        return false;
    }

    // 9. Return success status
    return true;
}

}  // namespace test_reaction
```

The implementation follows the required steps:

1. **Creates observer nodes**: Two `TestObserverClosure` nodes - a target and an observer
2. **Gets observer graph instance**: Uses the singleton `ObserverGraph::getInstance()`
3. **Adds observer relationship**: Uses `graph.addObserver()` to make observer observe target
4. **Notifies and verifies**: Calls `valueChanged()` on target and checks observer was notified
5. **Resets observer state**: Clears the changed flag and call count
6. **Closes observer node**: Uses `graph.closeNode()` to remove the observer from the graph
7. **Notifies target again**: Triggers another notification
8. **Verifies no notification**: Confirms observer was NOT notified after closure
9. **Returns success status**: Returns `true` if all checks pass, `false` otherwise

I also added a `reset()` method to the `TestObserverClosure` class to support step 5, as it's needed to clear the state between notifications.

