output.text:
Looking at the code fragments, I can see that:

1. `PersonRecord` should inherit from `reaction::FieldBase` and use `reaction::Var<T>` for reactive fields
2. The `field()` helper function is used to create reactive fields (from `FieldBase`)
3. To create a reactive variable of a custom class, use `reaction::var()`
4. To create a calculation depending on reactive fields, use `reaction::calc()`
5. Field values are updated using `.value()` method on the `Var<T>` fields

Here's the complete implementation:

```cpp
#pragma once

#include <reaction/reaction.h>
#include <string>

namespace test_reaction {

// ============================================
// Data model definitions
// ============================================

class PersonRecord : public reaction::FieldBase {
public:
    PersonRecord(const std::string& name, int age)
        : m_name(field(name)), m_age(field(age)) {
    }

    const std::string& getName() const {
        return m_name.get();
    }

    int getAge() const {
        return m_age.get();
    }

    void setName(const std::string& name) {
        m_name.value(name);
    }

    void setAge(int age) {
        m_age.value(age);
    }

private:
    reaction::Var<std::string> m_name;
    reaction::Var<int> m_age;
};

// ============================================
// Function implementations
// ============================================

/**
 * Function: Test reactive fields updating calculated info
 *
 * @param initialName Initial person name
 * @param initialAge Initial person age
 * @param newName Updated person name
 * @param newAge Updated person age
 * @return std::string Concatenated info after updates
 */
std::string test_reactive_fields(const std::string& initialName,
                                 int initialAge,
                                 const std::string& newName,
                                 int newAge) {
    // 1. Create reactive PersonRecord variable
    auto person = reaction::var(PersonRecord{initialName, initialAge});

    // 2. Create calculation depending on reactive fields
    std::string result;
    auto calc = reaction::calc([&result](const auto& p) {
        result = p.getName() + ":" + std::to_string(p.getAge());
        return result;
    }, person);

    // 3. Update name field
    person->setName(newName);

    // 4. Update age field
    person->setAge(newAge);

    // 5. Return updated info
    return result;
}

}  // namespace test_reaction
```

The implementation:
1. Creates a reactive `PersonRecord` using `reaction::var()`
2. Creates a calculation with `reaction::calc()` that builds a string from the person's name and age
3. Updates the name field using `setName()`, which internally calls `m_name.value(newName)`
4. Updates the age field using `setAge()`, which internally calls `m_age.value(newAge)`
5. Returns the concatenated info that was automatically updated by the reactive calculation

